  ($hk$أسبوع$hk$, $hk$week$hk$, $hk$usbuuʿ$hk$, $hk$Time$hk$, $hk$Said usbuuʿ, ending in ع. هالأسبوع this week; next week is الأسبوع الجاي. Plural أسابيع.$hk$, $hk$A week-view calendar page with seven boxes, Friday highlighted.$hk$, $hk$هالأسبوع عندي دوام$hk$, $hk$hal-usbuuʿ ʿindi dawaam$hk$, $hk$This week I have work$hk$, 9),
  ($hk$دايم$hk$, $hk$always$hk$, $hk$daayim$hk$, $hk$Time$hk$, $hk$Gulf says daayim (or دايماً) where MSA has دائماً with a hamza. Also 'permanent': سفرة دايمة — may your table always be laid — the blessing after a meal. على طول can also mean always.$hk$, $hk$A repeating loop icon over a daily coffee cup, the same scene four times.$hk$, $hk$دايم أشرب قهوة الصبح$hk$, $hk$daayim ashrab gahwa ṣ-ṣubḥ$hk$, $hk$I always drink coffee in the morning$hk$, 10),
  ($hk$أحياناً$hk$, $hk$sometimes$hk$, $hk$aḥyaanan$hk$, $hk$Time$hk$, $hk$Used as-is in Gulf speech, often as أحيان. The more colloquial مرات (some times) does the same job — مرات أروح, sometimes I go. Written with or without the final alif.$hk$, $hk$A calendar with a few random days ticked and the rest blank.$hk$, $hk$أحياناً الغدا بالليل$hk$, $hk$aḥyaanan il-ghada bil-layl$hk$, $hk$Sometimes lunch is at night$hk$, 11),
  ($hk$متى$hk$, $hk$when?$hk$, $hk$mita$hk$, $hk$Question$hk$, $hk$The when-question, said mita with a short i. Works with a thing or a verb: متى الغدا؟ متى تروح؟. Some Saudis say امتى. Not to be confused with يوم / لما, which mean 'when' in a story, not a question.$hk$, $hk$A man tapping his wrist where a watch would be, eyebrows raised in question.$hk$, $hk$متى الدوام؟$hk$, $hk$mita d-dawaam?$hk$, $hk$When is work?$hk$, 12),
  ($hk$الجمعة$hk$, $hk$Friday$hk$, $hk$il-jumʿa$hk$, $hk$Time$hk$, $hk$The day of the week that matters: prayer at noon, then the family lunch. Kuwaitis say il-yimʿa (ج → y). The other days: السبت, الأحد, الاثنين, الثلاثاء, الأربعا, الخميس — recognise them now, learn them in Stage 2.$hk$, $hk$A large family lunch on a floor sufra, a mosque visible through the window, midday light.$hk$, $hk$الجمعة الغدا عند أهلي$hk$, $hk$il-jumʿa l-ghada ʿind ahli$hk$, $hk$On Friday lunch is at my family's$hk$, 13),
  ($hk$الظهر$hk$, $hk$noon (the noon prayer time)$hk$, $hk$iẕ-ẕuhur$hk$, $hk$Time$hk$, $hk$Noon, named for the prayer. Said iẕ-ẕuhur with a helping vowel and the heavy ظ. Lunch is 'at ẕuhur' even when it is at three. بعد الظهر — afternoon.$hk$, $hk$The sun directly overhead over a mosque courtyard, short shadows.$hk$, $hk$الغدا الظهر$hk$, $hk$il-ghada ẕ-ẕuhur$hk$, $hk$Lunch is at noon$hk$, 14),
  ($hk$المغرب$hk$, $hk$sunset (the sunset prayer time)$hk$, $hk$il-maghrib$hk$, $hk$Time$hk$, $hk$Sunset, and the everyday appointment time: بعد المغرب, after maghrib, is when visits and outings start. Contains غ, the gargled g — different from the خ of خبز.$hk$, $hk$An orange sunset behind a minaret, lights coming on in the street.$hk$, $hk$نشوفك بعد المغرب$hk$, $hk$nshuufak baʿd il-maghrib$hk$, $hk$See you after maghrib$hk$, 15)
) AS v(word_arabic, word_english, transliteration, category, teaching_note, image_scene_description, example_arabic, example_transliteration, example_english, display_order), public.lessons l
WHERE l.source_key = $hk$gulf/s1/l10$hk$ AND w.lesson_id = l.id AND w.word_arabic = v.word_arabic;
