  ($hk$كوب$hk$, $hk$cup$hk$, $hk$kuub$hk$, $hk$Noun — café$hk$, $hk$A mug or paper cup: كوب كرك. The tiny handleless Arabic-coffee cup is a فنجان and a tea glass is a كاسة — three words for three drinks. The Egyptian كوباية is not Gulf.$hk$, $hk$A paper takeaway cup of milky tea with a lid, steam rising.$hk$, $hk$كوبين كرك، واحد بدون سكر$hk$, $hk$kuubayn karak, waaḥid biduun sukkar$hk$, $hk$Two cups of karak, one without sugar$hk$, 11),
  ($hk$صحن$hk$, $hk$plate$hk$, $hk$ṣaḥan$hk$, $hk$Noun — café$hk$, $hk$Two syllables in Gulf: ṣa-ḥan. Also a portion: صحن مندي 'a plate of mandi' is how you order. Plural صحون. Emphatic ص then a throaty ح.$hk$, $hk$A round plate heaped with rice and grilled chicken on a restaurant table.$hk$, $hk$صحن مندي دجاج لو سمحت$hk$, $hk$ṣaḥan mandi dijaaj law samaḥt$hk$, $hk$A plate of chicken mandi, please$hk$, 12),
  ($hk$شبعت$hk$, $hk$I'm full$hk$, $hk$shibaʿt$hk$, $hk$Verb phrase$hk$, $hk$Literally 'I have become full' — a past-tense verb, which is why it ends in -t. The only acceptable way to stop a host from serving more: والله شبعت. Feminine and masculine are the same.$hk$, $hk$A diner leaning back with a hand on their stomach, an empty plate in front.$hk$, $hk$خلاص، شبعت، الله يعافيك$hk$, $hk$khalaaṣ, shibaʿt, allah yʿaafiik$hk$, $hk$That's it, I'm full, thank you$hk$, 13),
  ($hk$مطعم$hk$, $hk$restaurant$hk$, $hk$maṭʿam$hk$, $hk$Place$hk$, $hk$Emphatic ط then ع back to back — a hard pair; go slowly: maṭ-ʿam. The café is كافيه or كوفي; a street stall is بسطة. Plural مطاعم.$hk$, $hk$A restaurant frontage at night with a lit sign, families entering.$hk$, $hk$هذا أحسن مطعم بالحي$hk$, $hk$haadha aḥsan maṭʿam bil-ḥayy$hk$, $hk$This is the best restaurant in the neighbourhood$hk$, 14),
  ($hk$كرك$hk$, $hk$karak (spiced milk tea)$hk$, $hk$karak$hk$, $hk$Food & drink$hk$, $hk$The Gulf's café drink — strong tea boiled with milk, sugar and cardamom, sold from roadside windows for a couple of riyals. Ordering one by name is the fastest way to sound at home.$hk$, $hk$A small paper cup of orange-brown milky tea at a roadside stall window.$hk$, $hk$نروح نشرب كرك؟$hk$, $hk$nruuḥ nishrab karak?$hk$, $hk$Shall we go and have a karak?$hk$, 15),
  ($hk$سفري$hk$, $hk$takeaway$hk$, $hk$safari$hk$, $hk$Noun — café$hk$, $hk$From سفر 'travel' — food that travels. The counter question everywhere: سفري ولا هنا؟ 'takeaway or here?'. The English تيك أواي is heard too.$hk$, $hk$A cashier holding up a paper bag with a question mark, a table behind.$hk$, $hk$سفري ولا هنا؟ — سفري$hk$, $hk$safari walla hina? — safari$hk$, $hk$Takeaway or eat in? — Takeaway$hk$, 16)
) AS v(word_arabic, word_english, transliteration, category, teaching_note, image_scene_description, example_arabic, example_transliteration, example_english, display_order), public.lessons l
WHERE l.source_key = $hk$gulf/s2/l06$hk$ AND w.lesson_id = l.id AND w.word_arabic = v.word_arabic;
