  ($hk$مكان$hk$, $hk$place$hk$, $hk$makaan$hk$, $hk$Noun$hk$, $hk$Place in every sense: المكان حلو (the place is nice), مكانك (in your place / instead of you). Plural أماكن. Restaurants and cafés are simply مكان in reviews.$hk$, $hk$A map pin dropped on a coastal café with palm trees.$hk$, $hk$المكان اللي تغدينا فيه أمس حلو$hk$, $hk$il-makaan illi tghaddayna fiih ams ḥilu$hk$, $hk$The place we had lunch at yesterday is nice$hk$, 5),
  ($hk$شي$hk$, $hk$thing$hk$, $hk$shay$hk$, $hk$Noun$hk$, $hk$The Gulf spelling and sound — shay, one syllable — never شيء. أي شي (anything), كل شي (everything), ما شي / ولا شي (nothing). Plural أشياء.$hk$, $hk$A tabletop with a phone, keys, a cup and a book scattered on it.$hk$, $hk$فيه شي غلط بالسيارة$hk$, $hk$fiih shay ghalaṭ bis-sayyaara$hk$, $hk$There's something wrong with the car$hk$, 6),
  ($hk$أحد$hk$, $hk$someone / anyone$hk$, $hk$aḥad$hk$, $hk$Pronoun$hk$, $hk$Mostly in questions and negatives: فيه أحد بالبيت؟ (is anyone home?), ما فيه أحد (nobody's there — also محد). Kuwaitis and Emiratis shorten it to حد. For a positive 'someone' people also say واحد (a guy).$hk$, $hk$A door slightly open with a shadow of a person visible behind it.$hk$, $hk$فيه أحد يعرف الطريق؟$hk$, $hk$fiih aḥad yaʿrif iṭ-ṭariig?$hk$, $hk$Is there anyone who knows the way?$hk$, 7),
  ($hk$أي أحد$hk$, $hk$anyone at all$hk$, $hk$ayy aḥad$hk$, $hk$Pronoun$hk$, $hk$أي (any) plus أحد: أي أحد يقدر (anyone can). Also أي واحد. The أي is short and the two words run together: ayy-aḥad.$hk$, $hk$An open call with many hands raised in a crowd.$hk$, $hk$أي أحد يقدر يجي، الباب مفتوح$hk$, $hk$ayy aḥad yigdar yiji, il-baab maftuuḥ$hk$, $hk$Anyone can come, the door is open$hk$, 8),
  ($hk$الكل$hk$, $hk$everyone$hk$, $hk$il-kill$hk$, $hk$Pronoun$hk$, $hk$'The all': الكل جا (everyone came), الكل يعرفه (everyone knows him). Also كلهم (all of them) and كل الناس. Gulf pronounces it kill with a short i.$hk$, $hk$A whole extended family gathered on the steps of a house for a photo.$hk$, $hk$الكل يعرف اللي صار$hk$, $hk$il-kill yaʿrif illi ṣaar$hk$, $hk$Everyone knows what happened$hk$, 9),
  ($hk$نوع$hk$, $hk$type / kind$hk$, $hk$nawʿ$hk$, $hk$Noun$hk$, $hk$أي نوع؟ (which kind?), نوع ثاني (another kind), من هالنوع (of this type). Plural أنواع, as in أنواع الناس (types of people), a favourite skit title.$hk$, $hk$Three different dates on a plate — one dark, one golden, one wrinkled — with small labels.$hk$, $hk$أي نوع تمر تبي؟$hk$, $hk$ayy nawʿ tamar tabi?$hk$, $hk$Which kind of dates do you want?$hk$, 10),
  ($hk$كل من$hk$, $hk$whoever / everyone who$hk$, $hk$kill min$hk$, $hk$Phrase$hk$, $hk$'Every who': كل من يشوفه يقول ما شاء الله (whoever sees him says mashallah). In fast speech bare اللي does the same: اللي يبي يجي. Do not confuse with كل من here as 'from every'.$hk$, $hk$A stream of people at a gate each receiving a date and a coffee cup from a host.$hk$, $hk$كل من جا العرس أكل$hk$, $hk$kill min ja l-ʿirs akal$hk$, $hk$Whoever came to the wedding ate$hk$, 11),
  ($hk$وين ما$hk$, $hk$wherever$hk$, $hk$wayn ma$hk$, $hk$Phrase$hk$, $hk$The question word وين plus ما: وين ما تروح (wherever you go). The same trick makes متى ما (whenever) and كيف ما / شلون ما (however). Said as one word: waynma.$hk$, $hk$A camel silhouette walking across three landscapes — desert, city, sea — in one strip.$hk$, $hk$وين ما تروح، بتلقى سعوديين$hk$, $hk$wayn ma truuḥ, bitilga saʿuudiyyiin$hk$, $hk$Wherever you go, you'll find Saudis$hk$, 12),
  ($hk$شغلة$hk$, $hk$thing / matter$hk$, $hk$shaghla$hk$, $hk$Noun$hk$, $hk$The Gulf's warm word for 'a thing': شغلة بسيطة (a small matter), فيه شغلة أبي أقولها لك (there's something I want to tell you). Plural شغلات. Different from شغل (work).$hk$, $hk$A woman leaning in to whisper to a friend, one hand cupped by her mouth.$hk$, $hk$فيه شغلة لازم أقولها لك$hk$, $hk$fiih shaghla laazim aguulha lik$hk$, $hk$There's something I have to tell you$hk$, 13),
  ($hk$ثاني$hk$, $hk$other / another / second$hk$, $hk$thaani$hk$, $hk$Adjective$hk$, $hk$واحد ثاني (another one), مرة ثانية (another time), الشي الثاني (the other thing). Feminine ثانية. The Gulf keeps the th sound — thaani, not taani.$hk$, $hk$Two identical white cups, one hand reaching for the second.$hk$, $hk$عطني واحد ثاني، هذا مكسور$hk$, $hk$ʿaṭni waaḥid thaani, haadha maksuur$hk$, $hk$Give me another one, this one is broken$hk$, 14),
  ($hk$ماركة$hk$, $hk$brand$hk$, $hk$maarka$hk$, $hk$Noun$hk$, $hk$A borrowed word everyone uses: أي ماركة؟ (which brand?), ماركة معروفة. Brand talk is a major Gulf pastime — cars, phones, perfume.$hk$, $hk$A row of perfume bottles with different logos on a glass shelf.$hk$, $hk$السيارة اللي تبيها أي ماركة؟$hk$, $hk$is-sayyaara lli tabiiha ayy maarka?$hk$, $hk$The car you want, which brand?$hk$, 15),
  ($hk$لون$hk$, $hk$colour$hk$, $hk$lawn$hk$, $hk$Noun$hk$, $hk$أي لون؟ (which colour?), لونه أبيض (it's white). Plural ألوان. Essential for describing a lost item or a car.$hk$, $hk$A paint colour chart fanned out in a hand.$hk$, $hk$الشنطة اللي ضاعت لونها أسود$hk$, $hk$ish-shanṭa lli ḍaaʿat lawnha aswad$hk$, $hk$The bag that got lost is black$hk$, 16),
  ($hk$شكل$hk$, $hk$shape / looks / appearance$hk$, $hk$shikil$hk$, $hk$Noun$hk$, $hk$Two syllables in the Gulf: shikil. شكله (he looks like / it seems) is a whole sentence starter: شكله تعبان (he looks tired), شكلها بتمطر (looks like rain).$hk$, $hk$A man squinting at a friend across the room, thinking 'looks tired'.$hk$, $hk$شكله من الكويت، مو كذا؟$hk$, $hk$shiklah min il-kwayt, mu kidha?$hk$, $hk$He looks like he's from Kuwait, doesn't he?$hk$, 17),
  ($hk$ضايع$hk$, $hk$lost (state)$hk$, $hk$ḍaayiʿ$hk$, $hk$Adjective$hk$, $hk$The participle of ضاع: جوالي ضايع (my phone is lost), آنا ضايع (I'm lost / I'm confused). Feminine ضايعة. The lost-and-found desk word.$hk$, $hk$A lost-property counter with a phone, a wallet and keys on a tray.$hk$, $hk$المفتاح اللي كان هني ضايع$hk$, $hk$il-miftaaḥ illi kaan hini ḍaayiʿ$hk$, $hk$The key that was here is lost$hk$, 18)
) AS v(word_arabic, word_english, transliteration, category, teaching_note, image_scene_description, example_arabic, example_transliteration, example_english, display_order), public.lessons l
WHERE l.source_key = $hk$gulf/s3/l05$hk$ AND w.lesson_id = l.id AND w.word_arabic = v.word_arabic;
