  ($hk$صباح الخير$hk$, $hk$good morning$hk$, $hk$ṣabaaḥ il-khayr$hk$, $hk$Greeting$hk$, $hk$Used from waking until lunch. The emphatic ص at the start is heavier than an English s. Sanaa elders stretch it to صبّحكم الله بالخير — same meaning, same reply pattern.$hk$, $hk$Sunrise over Sanaa rooftops, a man on a doorstep with a tea glass waving to a neighbour.$hk$, $hk$صباح الخير يا حاج$hk$, $hk$ṣabaaḥ il-khayr ya ḥaaj$hk$, $hk$Good morning, Hajj (respectful to an older man)$hk$, 4),
