  ($hk$مساء الخير$hk$, $hk$good evening$hk$, $hk$masaa il-khayr$hk$, $hk$Greeting$hk$, $hk$From late afternoon on — which in Sanaa is when the qat session breaks up and people are out again. Reply: مساء النور. The long Sanaa form is مسّيكم الله بالخير.$hk$, $hk$Dusk in an Old Sanaa lane, lamps lit in the tower-house windows, two friends greeting.$hk$, $hk$مساء الخير يا جماعة$hk$, $hk$masaa il-khayr ya jamaaʿa$hk$, $hk$Good evening, everyone$hk$, 6),
  ($hk$كيف حالك$hk$, $hk$how are you? (to a man)$hk$, $hk$kayf ḥaalak$hk$, $hk$Greeting$hk$, $hk$Yemen uses كيف, not the Gulf شلون or the Egyptian إزيك. To a woman Sanaa says كيف حالش — that -ish is the Sanaa signature. The shorter كيفك is also common.$hk$, $hk$Two men shaking right hands on a pavement, one leaning in with a questioning smile.$hk$, $hk$مرحبا، كيف حالك؟$hk$, $hk$marḥaba, kayf ḥaalak?$hk$, $hk$Hello, how are you?$hk$, 7),
  ($hk$بخير الحمد لله$hk$, $hk$I'm fine, thank God$hk$, $hk$bi-khayr il-ḥamdu lillaah$hk$, $hk$Reply$hk$, $hk$The only expected answer to كيف حالك, whatever your day was like. Say it, then return the question. The double l in الله is held longer and darker than an English l.$hk$, $hk$A smiling man with his right hand on his chest, nodding, in a sunny Sanaa lane.$hk$, $hk$بخير الحمد لله، وأنت؟$hk$, $hk$bi-khayr il-ḥamdu lillaah, w-ant?$hk$, $hk$Fine, thank God — and you?$hk$, 8),
  ($hk$ناهي$hk$, $hk$fine / good (Sanaa reply)$hk$, $hk$naahi$hk$, $hk$Reply$hk$, $hk$The word that marks a Sanaani. Where the rest of Yemen answers زين or طيب, old Sanaa says ناهي — 'fine, all well'. Recognise it now; you will hear it constantly in the old city and in comedy sketches imitating Sanaa speech.$hk$, $hk$An elderly Sanaa man in a shawl and janbiya nodding contentedly from a shop doorway.$hk$, $hk$كيف حالك؟ — ناهي الحمد لله$hk$, $hk$kayf ḥaalak? — naahi l-ḥamdu lillaah$hk$, $hk$How are you? — Fine, thank God$hk$, 9),
  ($hk$حياك الله$hk$, $hk$welcome$hk$, $hk$ḥayyaak allaah$hk$, $hk$Greeting$hk$, $hk$Literally 'may God give you life' — what a host says as you arrive and what a shopkeeper says as you walk in. Plural حياكم الله to a group or an elder. أهلاً وسهلاً is also heard and is the safe reply when it is said to you.$hk$, $hk$A host at an open door gesturing a guest inside with his right hand, warm light behind him.$hk$, $hk$حياك الله، تفضّل$hk$, $hk$ḥayyaak allaah, tfaḍḍal$hk$, $hk$Welcome — come in$hk$, 10),
