  ($hk$نسي$hk$, $hk$forgot$hk$, $hk$nisi$hk$, $hk$Verb$hk$, $hk$نسيت 'I forgot' is one of the most-used past verbs in any dialect. Weak verb like لقى: nisiit, nisyat 'she forgot'. Good stories start with a forgotten key.$hk$, $hk$A man outside a locked door patting his empty pockets.$hk$, $hk$نسيت المفتاح جوّا البيت$hk$, $hk$nisiit il-miftaaḥ jawwa l-bayt$hk$, $hk$I forgot the key inside the house$hk$, 8),
  ($hk$تذكّر$hk$, $hk$remember$hk$, $hk$tadhakkar$hk$, $hk$Verb$hk$, $hk$ذ is a real dh in Sanaa — tadhakkar, not tazakkar. تذكرت 'I remembered', تتذكر؟ 'do you remember?' opens a shared memory.$hk$, $hk$A woman with a finger raised and a lightbulb glow, an old wedding photo beside her.$hk$, $hk$تتذكر لما سرنا الحديدة؟$hk$, $hk$titdhakkar lamma sirna l-ḥudayda?$hk$, $hk$Do you remember when we went to Hodeidah?$hk$, 9),
  ($hk$ضحك$hk$, $hk$laughed$hk$, $hk$ḍiḥik$hk$, $hk$Verb$hk$, $hk$The reaction every story wants. ضحكنا 'we laughed', ضحّكني 'he made me laugh'. ض is the emphatic d.$hk$, $hk$Three men in a mafraj doubled over laughing, one slapping his knee.$hk$, $hk$ضحكنا لين وجعتنا بطوننا$hk$, $hk$ḍiḥikna liin wijʿatna buṭuunna$hk$, $hk$We laughed until our stomachs hurt$hk$, 10),
  ($hk$خايف$hk$, $hk$afraid$hk$, $hk$khaayif$hk$, $hk$Adjective$hk$, $hk$A participle: كنت خايف 'I was afraid'. Feminine خايفة, plural خايفين. خايف من 'afraid of'. Storytellers exaggerate it: كنت خايف موت 'scared to death'.$hk$, $hk$A boy hiding behind a door, eyes wide, a dark hallway beyond.$hk$, $hk$كنت خايف من الكلب$hk$, $hk$kunt khaayif min il-kalb$hk$, $hk$I was afraid of the dog$hk$, 11),
  ($hk$في الآخر$hk$, $hk$in the end$hk$, $hk$fil-aakhir$hk$, $hk$Connector$hk$, $hk$Signals the punchline is coming. Also بالآخر and آخر شي 'the last thing'. Learners often reach for MSA أخيراً — say في الآخر.$hk$, $hk$A finish line with a small flag; a relieved man crossing it.$hk$, $hk$في الآخر لقينا السيارة$hk$, $hk$fil-aakhir ligiina s-sayyaara$hk$, $hk$In the end we found the car$hk$, 12),
  ($hk$وقع$hk$, $hk$fell$hk$, $hk$wigiʿ$hk$, $hk$Verb$hk$, $hk$Half of all funny stories involve someone falling. وقعت 'I fell', وقع من فوق 'he fell from above'. Also 'it fell' for objects.$hk$, $hk$A man slipping on a wet step, tea glass flying.$hk$, $hk$وقع في الشارع والناس يضحكو$hk$, $hk$wigiʿ fish-shaariʿ win-naas yiḍḥaku$hk$, $hk$He fell in the street and people were laughing$hk$, 13),
  ($hk$هرب$hk$, $hk$ran away$hk$, $hk$harab$hk$, $hk$Verb$hk$, $hk$The exit from every scare story. هربت 'I ran away', هربو 'they fled'.$hk$, $hk$A cat sprinting out of a kitchen, a woman with a broom behind it.$hk$, $hk$شاف الكلب وهرب$hk$, $hk$shaaf il-kalb w-harab$hk$, $hk$He saw the dog and ran away$hk$, 14),
  ($hk$صرخ$hk$, $hk$screamed / shouted$hk$, $hk$ṣarakh$hk$, $hk$Verb$hk$, $hk$صرخت 'I screamed' — the moment of the story where the teller raises their voice to act it out.$hk$, $hk$A woman with hands up mid-scream, a cockroach on the wall.$hk$, $hk$صرخت وكل الحارة سمعت$hk$, $hk$ṣarakht w-kull il-ḥaara simʿat$hk$, $hk$I screamed and the whole neighbourhood heard$hk$, 15),
  ($hk$تصدّق$hk$, $hk$would you believe it?$hk$, $hk$tṣaddag$hk$, $hk$Expression$hk$, $hk$The storyteller's hook and the listener's reaction in one word. ق is g: tṣaddag. To a woman تصدّقي, to a group تصدّقو.$hk$, $hk$A man leaning in with raised eyebrows and an open palm, mid-sentence.$hk$, $hk$تصدّق؟ الكلب رجع البيت لحاله$hk$, $hk$tṣaddag? il-kalb rijiʿ il-bayt la-ḥaala$hk$, $hk$Would you believe it? The dog came home by itself$hk$, 16),
  ($hk$زعل$hk$, $hk$got upset$hk$, $hk$ziʿil$hk$, $hk$Verb$hk$, $hk$The emotional turn of a story: زعل مني 'he got upset with me'. زعلان is the state 'upset'. Not quite anger — that comes in Lesson 4.$hk$, $hk$A young man with crossed arms turning his face away from a friend.$hk$, $hk$زعلت مني لأني نسيت عيد ميلادها$hk$, $hk$ziʿlat minni laʾanni nisiit ʿiid miilaadha$hk$, $hk$She got upset with me because I forgot her birthday$hk$, 17)
) AS v(word_arabic, word_english, transliteration, category, teaching_note, image_scene_description, example_arabic, example_transliteration, example_english, display_order), public.lessons l
WHERE l.source_key = $hk$yemeni/s3/l01$hk$ AND w.lesson_id = l.id AND w.word_arabic = v.word_arabic;
